    "body": "## The issue\n\nIf you use `QueryArrayWidget` as the widget for a multi-value filter where **order matters** — most notably `OrderingFilter`, which supports multi-field sorting via `qs.order_by(*ordering)` — the order you submit values in isn't preserved. `?ordering[]=price&ordering[]=-name` (sort by price, then by name descending as a tiebreaker) can silently come back sorted as `-name,price` instead, with no error or warning — it just quietly sorts by the wrong field first.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`value_from_datadict` (`django_filters/widgets.py`) ends with:\n\n```python\nreturn list(set(ret))\n```\n\nConverting to a `set` before returning discards the order values were submitted in — Python sets aren't ordered by insertion:\n\n```python\n>>> list(set(['3', '1', '2', 'name', '-price']))\n['-price', '2', '3', '1', 'name']\n```\n\nThis is surprising for a widget documented as implementing \"request query array notation\" (`?foo[]=bar&foo[]=baz`) — array notation implies order is significant, and the docstring's own examples (`?foo=1&foo=2 -> foo = [1, 2]`) show an ordered result the actual return value doesn't honor.\n\n## Reproduced\n\nEnvironment: Python 3.13.3, Django 6.1, django-filter 26.1 (current `main`, commit `5128b8f`).\n\n```python\nimport django_filters\nfrom django_filters.widgets import QueryArrayWidget\n\nclass ProductFilter(django_filters.FilterSet):\n    ordering = django_filters.OrderingFilter(\n        fields=(\"price\", \"name\"),\n        widget=QueryArrayWidget,\n    )\n    class Meta:\n        model = Product\n        fields = []\n\n# Client requests: sort by price ascending, then name descending as a tiebreaker\nf = ProductFilter(data={\"ordering[]\": [\"price\", \"-name\"]}, queryset=Product.objects.all())\nprint(f.is_valid())\nprint(str(f.qs.query).split(\"ORDER BY\")[-1])\n```\n\nOutput on unpatched `main`:\n\n```\nTrue\n \"tests_product\".\"name\" DESC, \"tests_product\".\"price\" ASC\n```\n\nRequested `price, -name`; the generated `ORDER BY` came back as `name DESC, price ASC` — completely reversed. No error, no warning — the request just gets silently re-sorted by the wrong field first.\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nThe returned list should preserve the order values were submitted in, so the `ORDER BY` above should read `\"tests_product\".\"price\" ASC, \"tests_product\".\"name\" DESC`. Deduplication is fine (and already documented) — just not the reordering that comes along with it.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nDeduplicate while preserving order, e.g. `list(dict.fromkeys(ret))` instead of `list(set(ret))`.\n\n## Related\n\nDistinct from #1685 / #1686 (about the `MultiValueDict`/`QueryDict` detection branch earlier in the same method) — that fix doesn't touch the final `set()` call, so this would still reproduce afterward. Happy to send a PR for this one.",
